Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.92,0:00:04.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Toute votre vie, cette\Naiguille a indiqué le nord.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:04.59,0:00:08.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Mais les fonds marins ont\Nla mémoire plus longue.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:08.19,0:00:10.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Nord et sud se sont inversés.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:10.31,0:00:13.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Encore et encore, sans aucun calendrier.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:14.15,0:00:18.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Cette aiguille suit un {\c&H3468EB&}champ{\c&HF7F4F2&}\Nqui enveloppe toute la planète.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:18.55,0:00:20.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On le croirait permanent.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:20.38,0:00:21.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Il ne l'est pas.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:22.45,0:00:26.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Aux dorsales océaniques, la lave\Nfraîche refroidit et se fige en roche.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:26.65,0:00:30.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}De minuscules grains magnétiques y\Nenregistrent la direction du {\c&H3468EB&}champ{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:30.78,0:00:35.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Les fonds marins s'écartent, et chaque\Nrenversement ouvre une nouvelle bande.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:35.33,0:00:38.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Cinq millions d'années,\Nune vingtaine de renversements,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:38.44,0:00:40.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}et des intervalles extrêmement inégaux.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:42.05,0:00:43.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Alors, deux questions.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:43.39,0:00:45.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Quand aura lieu le prochain renversement ?
Dialogue: 0,0:00:45.94,0:00:48.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Et {\c&H00A1ED&}à quelle fréquence{\c&HF7F4F2&} le\Nchamp se renverse-t-il ?
Dialogue: 0,0:00:48.92,0:00:50.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}À laquelle peut-on répondre ?
Dialogue: 0,0:00:53.08,0:00:57.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Dans un modèle numérique simple\Nd'un {\c&H3468EB&}champ{\c&HF7F4F2&} qui se renverse,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:57.12,0:00:59.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}la première est sans espoir.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:59.34,0:01:00.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}La seconde, non.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:01.38,0:01:06.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Pour comprendre pourquoi, nous passerons\Npar le métal liquide et le chaos.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:06.10,0:01:10.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Puis par des {\c&HF09CB4&}machines{\c&HF7F4F2&} qui imaginent\Ndes instantanés de champs magnétiques.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:10.82,0:01:16.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Elles doivent obéir exactement à certaines\Nlois, et à d'autres seulement en moyenne.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:17.59,0:01:20.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}À quelle fréquence le\N{\c&H3468EB&}champ{\c&HF7F4F2&} se renverse-t-il ?
Dialogue: 0,0:01:20.44,0:01:22.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Une carte pour s'initier au projet MilaDy,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:22.94,0:01:26.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}de la boussole jusqu'à la\Nrecherche d'aujourd'hui.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:28.92,0:01:31.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Les premières simulations\Ndoivent-elles viser
Dialogue: 0,0:01:31.30,0:01:33.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}{\c&H00A1ED&}la fréquence{\c&HF7F4F2&} des renversements du champ,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:33.41,0:01:36.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}ou les signes annonciateurs\Njuste avant un renversement ?
Dialogue: 0,0:01:41.92,0:01:45.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Y a-t-il un aimant géant caché\Nà l'intérieur de la Terre ?
Dialogue: 0,0:01:46.00,0:01:50.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Tout au fond, le noyau externe\Nest un océan de fer liquide.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:50.15,0:01:55.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Livré à lui-même, un {\c&H3468EB&}champ{\c&HF7F4F2&} s'y éteindrait\Nen quelques dizaines de milliers d'années.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:56.06,0:02:01.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Pourtant, le champ de la Terre existe\Ndepuis plusieurs milliards d'années.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:01.27,0:02:05.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Le fer en mouvement doit donc\Nle fabriquer, en permanence.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:06.55,0:02:11.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Prenez un barreau aimanté :\Nle nord à un bout, le sud à l'autre.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:11.43,0:02:12.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Coupez-le en deux.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:12.81,0:02:17.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Vous obtenez deux aimants,\Nqui ont chacun un nord et un sud.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:17.38,0:02:21.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Personne n'a jamais trouvé\Nde pôle magnétique isolé.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:22.25,0:02:27.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Les {\c&H3468EB&}lignes de champ{\c&HF7F4F2&} disent la même\Nchose : elles n'ont ni début ni fin.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:27.55,0:02:31.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Tracez une boîte,\Nn'importe laquelle, et comptez.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:31.21,0:02:34.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Il entre autant de lignes qu'il en sort.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:34.20,0:02:36.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Gardez cette boîte en tête.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:37.42,0:02:43.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Une dynamo de vélo transforme le mouvement\Nen électricité, grâce à un aimant intégré.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:43.12,0:02:47.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}La Terre, elle, doit fabriquer\Nle sien. L'astuce : une boucle.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:48.18,0:02:52.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Approchez un micro du haut-parleur\Nqui le diffuse, et montez le {\c&H7AAF1B&}gain{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:52.61,0:02:55.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}En dessous d'un certain point,\Nun murmure s'éteint.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:55.89,0:02:58.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Au-delà, la boucle s'alimente elle-même.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:01.36,0:03:04.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Faites maintenant tourner\Nun disque de cuivre,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:04.67,0:03:08.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}relié à une bobine\Nenroulée autour de son axe.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:08.28,0:03:12.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Le courant crée le {\c&H3468EB&}champ{\c&HF7F4F2&},\Net le champ crée le courant.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:12.30,0:03:16.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Faites-le tourner de plus en\Nplus vite. Le champ grandit-il
Dialogue: 0,0:03:16.58,0:03:21.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}petit à petit, ou pas du tout\Navant une certaine vitesse ?
Dialogue: 0,0:03:24.98,0:03:26.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Pas du tout, jusqu'à un seuil.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:26.95,0:03:30.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Au-delà, chaque tour multiplie\Nun minuscule {\c&H3468EB&}champ germe{\c&HF7F4F2&}
Dialogue: 0,0:03:30.43,0:03:34.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}par le même facteur : c'est\Nune croissance exponentielle.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:34.35,0:03:37.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Juste au-dessus du seuil,\Nla croissance est lente.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:37.62,0:03:41.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Puis le champ freine le disque,\Net la croissance s'arrête.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:42.82,0:03:44.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Qui paie la facture ?
Dialogue: 0,0:03:44.17,0:03:48.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Un moteur entraîne l'{\c&HE58739&}écoulement{\c&HF7F4F2&} ;\Ndans la Terre, c'est la convection.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:48.62,0:03:52.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}L'écoulement étire le champ ;\Nle champ freine l'écoulement.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:52.42,0:03:55.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Une fois la croissance\Narrêtée, chaque watt qui
Dialogue: 0,0:03:55.33,0:03:58.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}entre ressort sous forme\Nde chaleur, en moyenne.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:58.60,0:04:03.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Une dynamo transforme le mouvement en\Nchamp ; elle ne crée aucune énergie.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:04.55,0:04:09.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Le {\c&H3468EB&}champ{\c&HF7F4F2&} de la Terre pointe aujourd'hui\Ndans un sens. Lequel devrait-il préférer ?
Dialogue: 0,0:04:13.30,0:04:18.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Inversez chaque ligne de champ : les\Néquations de la dynamo restent les mêmes.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:18.96,0:04:24.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Donc, aucun des deux. Ce sont des jumeaux\Nen miroir, tout aussi permis par les lois.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:25.07,0:04:28.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Le sens qu'on obtient\Nrelève de l'histoire,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:28.10,0:04:32.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}pas des lois : dans le disque,\Nle signe du germe décide.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:32.34,0:04:35.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Et rien dans les lois\Nn'interdit un renversement.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:37.49,0:04:42.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Du fer en mouvement, une boucle avec\Nun seuil, aucun sens privilégié.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:42.62,0:04:46.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Mais le disque avait des fils ;\Nle fer liquide n'en a aucun.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:47.08,0:04:51.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Alors, quel mouvement en est\Ncapable, et à quelle vitesse ?
Dialogue: 0,0:04:52.95,0:04:56.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Près du seuil, les simulations\Nde dynamo doivent-elles
Dialogue: 0,0:04:56.34,0:05:00.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}partir d'un germe minuscule,\Nd'un germe fort, ou des deux ?
Dialogue: 0,0:05:05.95,0:05:09.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Quel mouvement peut faire\Ncroître un {\c&H3468EB&}champ magnétique{\c&HF7F4F2&} ?
Dialogue: 0,0:05:09.65,0:05:11.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Dans un conducteur parfait,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:11.39,0:05:15.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}les lignes de champ seraient\Ndes fils gelés dans le fluide.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:15.49,0:05:19.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Partout où l'{\c&HE58739&}écoulement{\c&HF7F4F2&} étire\Nun fil, le champ s'y renforce.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:20.42,0:05:25.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Dans un vrai métal, les fils s'estompent,\Ncomme de l'encre qui s'étale.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:25.37,0:05:30.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}La vitesse de cet estompage est fixée\Npar la {\c&H7AAF1B&}diffusivité magnétique{\c&HF7F4F2&} du métal.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:30.75,0:05:32.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Même écoulement, deux métaux.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:32.77,0:05:37.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Dans un bon conducteur, l'estompage\Nest lent : les fils restent nets.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:37.59,0:05:42.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Dans un mauvais, il est rapide :\Nles fils se fondent dans le flou.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:43.39,0:05:47.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Un seul nombre compte les points :\Nle {\c&H7AAF1B&}nombre de Reynolds magnétique{\c&HF7F4F2&}, Rm.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:47.99,0:05:49.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}C'est la {\c&HE58739&}vitesse de l'écoulement{\c&HF7F4F2&},
Dialogue: 0,0:05:50.02,0:05:54.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}multipliée par sa taille, divisée\Npar la {\c&H7AAF1B&}diffusivité magnétique{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:54.26,0:05:57.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}À grand Rm, l'étirement va\Nplus vite que l'estompage.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:57.65,0:05:59.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}À petit Rm, l'estompage l'emporte.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:00.73,0:06:05.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Tournez le bouton pour augmenter Rm,\Net observez un faible champ germe.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:05.81,0:06:07.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}À petit Rm, le germe s'éteint.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:07.96,0:06:11.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Au-delà d'un seuil, le taux\Nde croissance franchit zéro,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:11.84,0:06:14.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}et le germe grandit, comme le larsen.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:15.89,0:06:19.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Les dynamos de laboratoire\Nutilisent du sodium liquide.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:19.73,0:06:22.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Il coule aussi facilement que l'eau.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:22.24,0:06:27.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Mais un champ s'y estompe environ cent\Nmille fois plus vite que ses tourbillons.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:27.82,0:06:31.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Le rapport de ces deux\Nvitesses, un sur cent mille,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:31.25,0:06:34.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}c'est le {\c&H7AAF1B&}nombre de Prandtl magnétique{\c&HF7F4F2&}, Pm.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:35.28,0:06:39.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Pour atteindre un Rm de quelques\Ndizaines, il faut brasser violemment.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:39.87,0:06:42.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Le {\c&H7AAF1B&}nombre de Reynolds{\c&HF7F4F2&} de l'écoulement,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:42.27,0:06:46.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}la jauge de la turbulence,\Nse chiffre alors en millions.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:46.10,0:06:49.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Toute dynamo de laboratoire\Nest donc turbulente.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:50.45,0:06:53.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Seules trois expériences au\Nsodium liquide ont fabriqué
Dialogue: 0,0:06:53.85,0:06:57.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}leur propre champ, chaque\Nfois d'un genre différent.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:57.37,0:07:00.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}À Riga, un écoulement\Nen tire-bouchon a fait
Dialogue: 0,0:07:00.08,0:07:03.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}naître un champ qui oscille\Net tourne lentement.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:03.33,0:07:06.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}À Karlsruhe, cinquante-deux\Ncellules en spirale ont
Dialogue: 0,0:07:06.47,0:07:09.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}produit un champ stationnaire,\Nproche d'un {\c&H3468EB&}dipôle{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:09.86,0:07:15.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Et seule l'expérience VKS, à Cadarache,\Na donné un dipôle capable de se renverser.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:16.01,0:07:19.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Une quatrième, DRESDYN, à Dresde,\Ncherche à les rejoindre :
Dialogue: 0,0:07:19.56,0:07:23.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}un cylindre en rotation\Ndont l'axe tourne lentement.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:24.19,0:07:27.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}À l'intérieur de VKS :\Nun cylindre qui contient
Dialogue: 0,0:07:27.18,0:07:29.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}cent cinquante litres de sodium liquide.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:30.03,0:07:35.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}À ses extrémités, deux disques à pales,\Nles turbines, tournent en sens opposés.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:36.18,0:07:39.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Le métal des disques devrait-il\Navoir de l'importance ?
Dialogue: 0,0:07:42.87,0:07:45.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Avec des disques en acier inoxydable,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:45.58,0:07:49.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}il n'y a eu aucune dynamo,\Nmême à pleine vitesse.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:49.47,0:07:55.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Remplacez-les par des disques en fer doux,\Net un dipôle grandit le long de l'axe.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:57.51,0:08:02.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Le seuil ne dépend donc pas que de\NRm : les matériaux comptent aussi.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:04.05,0:08:08.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Faites maintenant tourner les deux\Ndisques à des vitesses différentes.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:08.62,0:08:11.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Pour certains réglages,\Nle dipôle se renverse
Dialogue: 0,0:08:11.43,0:08:14.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}encore et encore, sans aucun calendrier.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:14.23,0:08:19.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Comme l'aiguille de la boussole du début,\Nmais toutes les une à deux minutes.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:20.16,0:08:23.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Chaque renversement, lui,\Nne dure qu'environ cinq secondes.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:23.77,0:08:26.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Des bandes inégales,\Ncomme celles des fonds marins,
Dialogue: 0,0:08:26.78,0:08:30.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}mais écrites en minutes,\Npas en millions d'années.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:31.32,0:08:35.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Impossible de simuler le tout petit\N{\c&H7AAF1B&}Pm{\c&HF7F4F2&} du sodium : quelles valeurs
Dialogue: 0,0:08:35.49,0:08:39.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}de Pm couvrir dans les premières\Nsimulations en trois dimensions ?
Dialogue: 0,0:08:45.55,0:08:50.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Riga, Karlsruhe et VKS ont couronné\Nune traque de près d'un siècle.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:50.74,0:08:52.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}En voici la carte.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:53.03,0:08:55.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Première piste, les archives de la Terre :
Dialogue: 0,0:08:55.67,0:08:59.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}des laves aimantées à l'envers,\Nmille neuf cent six.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:59.23,0:09:03.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Les bandes des fonds marins,\Nmille neuf cent soixante-trois.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:03.15,0:09:06.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Puis les renversements vus\Ncomme des événements aléatoires,
Dialogue: 0,0:09:06.87,0:09:09.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}dont la probabilité\Nchange au fil des âges.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:11.09,0:09:12.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Deuxième piste, la théorie.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:12.73,0:09:15.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Mille neuf cent dix-neuf :\Nun fluide conducteur en
Dialogue: 0,0:09:15.64,0:09:18.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}mouvement pourrait entretenir\Nle champ du Soleil.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:18.80,0:09:21.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Mille neuf cent trente-trois :\Naucun écoulement ne
Dialogue: 0,0:09:21.72,0:09:25.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}peut entretenir un champ\Nsymétrique autour d'un axe.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:25.96,0:09:27.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Mille neuf cent cinquante-huit :
Dialogue: 0,0:09:27.73,0:09:31.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}les premiers exemples démontrés\Nd'écoulements qui en sont capables.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:31.72,0:09:35.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Dans les années soixante-dix arrivent\Ndes écoulements dynamos simples,
Dialogue: 0,0:09:35.62,0:09:38.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}qui deviendront les plans\Nde Riga et de Karlsruhe.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:39.89,0:09:42.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Troisième piste : construire une dynamo.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:42.33,0:09:44.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Mille neuf cent soixante-trois :
Dialogue: 0,0:09:44.19,0:09:47.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}une maquette de dynamo à\Ncylindres de fer en rotation.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:47.64,0:09:49.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Mille neuf cent quatre-vingt-dix-neuf :
Dialogue: 0,0:09:49.92,0:09:53.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}le sodium fabrique son propre\Nchamp, à Riga, puis à Karlsruhe.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:54.76,0:09:57.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Deux mille six : dans VKS,\Nl'effet dynamo n'apparaît
Dialogue: 0,0:09:57.83,0:10:01.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}qu'avec des disques en fer ;\Npuis viennent les premiers
Dialogue: 0,0:10:01.08,0:10:04.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}renversements d'une dynamo\Nturbulente au sodium.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:05.82,0:10:07.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Quatrième piste, les ordinateurs.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:07.68,0:10:09.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Mille neuf cent quatre-vingt-un :
Dialogue: 0,0:10:09.43,0:10:12.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}une turbulence simulée\Nfait naître des dynamos.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:12.23,0:10:14.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Mille neuf cent quatre-vingt-quinze :
Dialogue: 0,0:10:14.21,0:10:17.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}dans un noyau terrestre simulé,\Nle champ se renverse.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:17.35,0:10:21.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Les repères cerclés signalent les articles\Ncosignés par les encadrants du projet.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:21.90,0:10:24.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Mille neuf cent quatre-vingt-dix-sept :\Nen simulation,
Dialogue: 0,0:10:24.82,0:10:28.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}deux tourbillons contrarotatifs\Nproduisent un effet dynamo.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:28.31,0:10:32.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Deux mille cinq : ces dynamos fonctionnent\Nencore pour un {\c&H7AAF1B&}Pm{\c&HF7F4F2&} d'un centième.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:33.43,0:10:35.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}À partir de deux mille seize,
Dialogue: 0,0:10:35.25,0:10:39.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}deux codes simulent la cuve de\NVKS, turbines en fer comprises.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:39.44,0:10:43.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Deux mille vingt-six : tous deux\Nobtiennent des renversements ;
Dialogue: 0,0:10:43.45,0:10:48.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}dans les cas explorés, seulement avec\Nun milieu conducteur tout autour.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:48.27,0:10:50.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Leur simulation de référence :
Dialogue: 0,0:10:50.16,0:10:54.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}un {\c&H7AAF1B&}nombre de Reynolds{\c&HF7F4F2&} d'environ\Nmille six cents, et non des millions.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:55.99,0:10:59.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Cinquième piste : quelques\Ncadrans au lieu d'une cuve.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:59.44,0:11:01.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Mille neuf cent cinquante-huit :
Dialogue: 0,0:11:01.39,0:11:04.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}deux dynamos à disque,\Ncouplées, se renversent déjà.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:04.86,0:11:08.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Deux mille douze : trois\Ncadrans qui se renversent sous
Dialogue: 0,0:11:08.26,0:11:12.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}le seul effet du chaos ;\Nnous jouerons bientôt avec eux.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:12.91,0:11:15.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Deux mille vingt-six :\Nquatre cadrans ajustés sur
Dialogue: 0,0:11:15.93,0:11:17.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}les renversements simulés de VKS,
Dialogue: 0,0:11:17.96,0:11:21.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}par une méthode qui découvre\Nles équations dans les données.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:23.15,0:11:27.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}MilaDy, lancé en deux mille vingt-six,\Napporte l'apprentissage automatique
Dialogue: 0,0:11:27.47,0:11:31.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}là où se rencontrent les cuves,\Nles ordinateurs et les cadrans.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:31.45,0:11:35.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Pourtant, la piste des données reste\Nmince : en deux mille vingt-deux,
Dialogue: 0,0:11:35.60,0:11:38.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}les méthodes d'apprentissage\Nessayées par une étude n'ont
Dialogue: 0,0:11:38.97,0:11:42.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}pas su prévoir de façon fiable\Nun renversement géomagnétique.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:43.78,0:11:47.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Le stage et un projet de\Nthèse y ajoutent un pari :
Dialogue: 0,0:11:47.59,0:11:53.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}des {\c&HF09CB4&}machines{\c&HF7F4F2&} qui imaginent des champs\Nde dynamo et obéissent à leur physique.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:54.54,0:11:58.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Derrière chaque piste, la même course :\Nl'étirement contre l'estompage.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:02.65,0:12:07.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Back to the race: the {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&} stretches\Nthe field, and diffusion smudges it.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:07.96,0:12:12.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Physicists write the very same\Nrace as a single equation.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:13.01,0:12:17.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On the left: how fast the\N{\c&H3468EB&}magnetic field{\c&HF7F4F2&} changes, at each point.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:17.59,0:12:20.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}First term on the right:\Nthe flow carries the
Dialogue: 0,0:12:20.71,0:12:24.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}field along and stretches\Nit, like the threads.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:24.26,0:12:29.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Second term: diffusion smudges it,\Nat a pace set by the magnetic diffusivity.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:29.78,0:12:31.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A poor conductor smudges fast.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:31.93,0:12:36.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Stretching against smudging:\Nthe whole race, in one line.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:37.22,0:12:42.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Picture the {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&} as a conveyor\Nbelt that does not feel its cargo.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:42.38,0:12:48.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A weak {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} rides on the imposed flow,\Nand fades or grows exponentially.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:48.17,0:12:53.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On a log scale: a straight line,\Nwhose slope is the growth rate.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:54.15,0:12:57.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But a strong field\Npushes back on the flow,
Dialogue: 0,0:12:57.41,0:13:02.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}as it braked the copper disc:\Nthe line bends into a plateau.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:02.25,0:13:05.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}These are the kinematic\Nand dynamic regimes.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:05.68,0:13:07.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Flips live on the plateau.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:08.92,0:13:12.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}To test ideas, physicists\Nneed a {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&} simple enough
Dialogue: 0,0:13:12.90,0:13:16.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}to write on one line,\Nyet able to make a dynamo.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:16.95,0:13:19.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One classic choice: the ABC flow.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:20.51,0:13:23.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It fills space with\Nidentical twisting rooms.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:23.61,0:13:26.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three crossed winds make\Nthe fluid corkscrew:
Dialogue: 0,0:13:26.58,0:13:29.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}it spins around the direction it moves.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:29.46,0:13:32.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}This box returns higher on our climb.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:33.22,0:13:38.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now turn one {\c&H7AAF1B&}knob{\c&HF7F4F2&} on this flow: Rm.\NTurning it up means less diffusion.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:38.46,0:13:41.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Less smudging should\Nonly help the stretching.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:42.75,0:13:46.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So once the dynamo switches\Non, it stays on. Right?
Dialogue: 0,0:13:50.76,0:13:54.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Here is the growth rate,\Ncomputed at twenty-four settings.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:54.72,0:14:00.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Below about nine, every {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} fades. Then\Ngrowth turns positive: the dynamo is on.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:00.32,0:14:03.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Near seventeen and a half,\Nit switches off again.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:03.67,0:14:06.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Above about twenty-seven, it comes back.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:06.41,0:14:11.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Not one threshold, but windows: turning\Na knob further does not always help.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:11.60,0:14:14.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It agrees with published\Nresults. No machine
Dialogue: 0,0:14:14.48,0:14:17.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}learning yet: this only tests the solver.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:18.89,0:14:22.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Real dynamos have not\None {\c&H7AAF1B&}knob{\c&HF7F4F2&}, but a panel.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:22.32,0:14:26.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}How hard the metal is stirred;\Nhow well it conducts.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:26.37,0:14:29.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}What the discs are made\Nof: remember the iron.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:29.96,0:14:34.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}How unequal their speeds are:\Nthat is when VKS flipped.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:34.25,0:14:38.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For DRESDYN, a precessing tank:\Nhow fast its axis turns.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:39.51,0:14:43.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Even the wall counts: in VKS\Nsimulations, changing only its
Dialogue: 0,0:14:43.14,0:14:47.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}magnetic condition raised the\Nthreshold by about forty percent.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:48.52,0:14:53.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Every turbulent dynamo simulation\Nin three dimensions is very costly.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:53.51,0:14:56.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So only a handful of knob\Nsettings get simulated.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:57.05,0:15:02.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each one is a mountain of data: a single\Nsnapshot can weigh more than a gigabyte.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:03.80,0:15:06.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each run holds only a few flips.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:06.50,0:15:10.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And between the dots,\Nthere is nothing at all.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:11.59,0:15:14.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Those gaps are where this film lives.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:14.37,0:15:17.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The study behind this\Nfilm asks three things.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:18.65,0:15:22.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One: can a {\c&HF09CB4&}machine{\c&HF7F4F2&} learn\Nthe whole family of fields,
Dialogue: 0,0:15:22.62,0:15:25.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}even at knob settings between the dots?
Dialogue: 0,0:15:25.90,0:15:29.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Two: can it respect\Neach law where it holds,
Dialogue: 0,0:15:29.27,0:15:33.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}exactly in every field,\Nor {\c&H00A1ED&}on average{\c&HF7F4F2&} over many fields?
Dialogue: 0,0:15:33.78,0:15:37.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three: can it tell us\N{\c&H00A1ED&}how often{\c&HF7F4F2&} the field flips?
Dialogue: 0,0:15:38.46,0:15:41.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}To find out, the study's\Nplan climbs a staircase,
Dialogue: 0,0:15:41.86,0:15:45.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}from three numbers to the\Nshapes of real experiments.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:46.75,0:15:50.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One honest note: this\Nindependent study is prospective,
Dialogue: 0,0:15:50.19,0:15:53.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}built on small experiments\Non an ordinary computer,
Dialogue: 0,0:15:53.38,0:15:57.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and not endorsed by MilaDy,\Nits partners or the supervisors.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:59.25,0:16:03.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}An equation, windows, a panel\Nof knobs, a handful of dots.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:03.53,0:16:05.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Full simulations cost too much,
Dialogue: 0,0:16:05.74,0:16:10.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}so we start with a three-dial\Ncaricature that already flips.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:10.31,0:16:12.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Can anyone say when it flips next?
Dialogue: 0,0:16:14.32,0:16:17.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}With only a handful of runs,\Nshould the {\c&H7AAF1B&}settings{\c&HF7F4F2&}
Dialogue: 0,0:16:17.66,0:16:21.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}be spread evenly,\Nor packed near each threshold?
Dialogue: 0,0:16:26.72,0:16:30.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Here is the caricature:\Nthree dials, linked by springs.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:30.88,0:16:34.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One is the {\c&H3468EB&}dipole{\c&HF7F4F2&},\Nthe north-south part of the field.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:34.89,0:16:37.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One is the {\c&H3468EB&}quadrupole{\c&HF7F4F2&}, a four-pole part.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:37.92,0:16:41.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The third is a {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&} pattern,\Ndriven by a knob, Gamma.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:42.83,0:16:46.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three short equations, from\NGissinger, tie them together.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:46.62,0:16:50.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Flip both field dials,\Nand the equations read the same.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:50.28,0:16:54.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three dials, three numbers:\Nthe bottom step of the staircase.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:55.52,0:16:59.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Use two dials as a map:\N{\c&H3468EB&}quadrupole{\c&HF7F4F2&} across, dipole up.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:59.36,0:17:03.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}With the knob low, the state\Ncircles in one whirlpool, and stays.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:04.07,0:17:08.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A mirror twin circles the\Nmirror whirlpool: dipole down.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:08.12,0:17:11.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each start is trapped.\NThe dipole never changes sign.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:12.86,0:17:14.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now turn {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&} up.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:14.16,0:17:19.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Just above zero point eight six, the\Nwhirlpools touch: a narrow corridor opens.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:19.85,0:17:24.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The state lingers on one side,\Nthen slips through. The {\c&H3468EB&}dipole{\c&HF7F4F2&} flips.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:24.76,0:17:29.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Nothing here is random. So,\Nwill the flips come like clockwork?
Dialogue: 0,0:17:32.70,0:17:35.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No. Long waits and\Nshort ones, irregularly.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:35.47,0:17:39.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The dipole's sign prints uneven\Nstripes, like the seafloor.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:40.49,0:17:44.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now count: one tick each\Ntime the {\c&H3468EB&}dipole{\c&HF7F4F2&} crosses zero.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:44.71,0:17:50.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Not every tick is a flip: an excursion\Ncan cross and come straight back.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:50.35,0:17:53.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Ticks per unit of time: the {\c&H00A1ED&}crossing rate{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:54.54,0:17:57.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Up to zero point eight six: silence.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:57.28,0:18:01.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Just above, about two ticks\Nper thousand time units.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:01.25,0:18:04.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&} equal to one, about eleven.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:05.19,0:18:10.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Set {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&} at zero point nine, and\Nlaunch two copies, one millionth apart.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:11.03,0:18:17.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}We know the state to six decimals.\NCan we predict the date of the next flip?
Dialogue: 0,0:18:21.01,0:18:23.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At first, the copies move as one.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:23.36,0:18:27.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But their gap nearly triples\Nevery eleven time units.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:27.14,0:18:31.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}After about a hundred and fifty,\Nthey disagree completely.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:32.18,0:18:36.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The average wait between crossings\Nis longer: over two hundred.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:36.33,0:18:40.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each extra decimal buys only\Nabout twenty-five more time units.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:40.47,0:18:45.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So the forecast fades before the average\Nwait ends. Only the statistics survive.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:46.95,0:18:50.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The path is lost,\Nso leave the shutter open.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:50.46,0:18:54.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each pass leaves a little\Nlight: a glow builds.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:54.20,0:18:59.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A copy started on the other side\Nfirst paints the mirror image.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:59.21,0:19:02.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Given time, both paint the same glow.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:03.06,0:19:07.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Where does the {\c&H3468EB&}dipole{\c&HF7F4F2&} spend its\Ntime? Two humps, the same for both.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:07.54,0:19:11.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}This reproducible glow is\Nthe stationary distribution.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:11.16,0:19:15.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Below the threshold, each\Nstart paints its own whirlpool.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:16.19,0:19:20.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now film the trajectory,\None frame every two time units.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:20.32,0:19:22.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Neighbouring frames look alike.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:22.60,0:19:24.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Cut the film into frames.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:24.44,0:19:28.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Drop them in a shoebox, shake,\Nand throw away the timestamps.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:28.94,0:19:32.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Pour the box out:\Nthe frames redraw the same glow.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:33.53,0:19:37.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each frame is a snapshot:\Nthree dial readings, nothing more.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:37.54,0:19:40.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It carries no timestamp and no time order.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:40.36,0:19:44.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Yet the shoebox, plus the equations,\Nanswers many questions.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:44.38,0:19:48.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Averages, energy budgets, even\Nhow often the dipole crosses zero.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:49.92,0:19:54.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Pick one photo. Its three\Ndial readings give its energy.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:54.08,0:19:59.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}From that photo alone, the equations\Ntell how fast its energy changes.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:59.28,0:20:02.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Drop one marble per photo at that value.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:03.15,0:20:08.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Some photos are gaining energy,\Nothers losing it. The pile spreads wide.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:08.40,0:20:10.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Yet its average sits at zero.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:10.51,0:20:13.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Energy in equals energy out, on average.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:14.32,0:20:17.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Like a bank account: over the years,
Dialogue: 0,0:20:17.14,0:20:21.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}income matches spending,\Nyet each day gains or loses.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:21.58,0:20:27.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}This is a {\c&H00A1ED&}law of the crowd{\c&HF7F4F2&}. It holds\Nfor the shoebox, not for each photo.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:28.79,0:20:32.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}How many independent snapshots\Ndoes each setting need,
Dialogue: 0,0:20:32.51,0:20:35.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and how far apart must they be saved?
Dialogue: 0,0:20:40.75,0:20:44.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A puzzle. How many cars\Ncross this line each hour?
Dialogue: 0,0:20:44.60,0:20:50.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No clock. Just one photo from above,\Nwhere every speedometer can be read.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:51.11,0:20:56.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Can one photo, with no time\Nin it, give you a rate?
Dialogue: 0,0:20:59.44,0:21:02.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It can. Draw a thin strip around the line.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:02.80,0:21:08.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A fast car spends little time inside,\Nso catching one there counts for more.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:09.79,0:21:15.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Add up the speeds of the cars inside,\Nand divide by the strip's width.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:15.59,0:21:19.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On average, that is how\Nmany cars cross per hour.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:20.85,0:21:26.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Back to the shoebox. Spread its\Nphotos out by their {\c&H3468EB&}dipole{\c&HF7F4F2&} reading.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:26.27,0:21:30.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The line to cross is zero:\Ndraw a thin strip around it.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:31.62,0:21:34.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each photo also needs a speedometer.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:34.25,0:21:37.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No clock required:\Nthe dipole's equation turns
Dialogue: 0,0:21:37.51,0:21:40.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the photo's three dial\Nreadings into a speed.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:40.99,0:21:44.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Up or down, both count,\Nso keep only its size.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:45.26,0:21:47.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Add the speeds inside the strip,
Dialogue: 0,0:21:47.57,0:21:52.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}then divide by its width and\Nby the total number of photos.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:52.14,0:21:54.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Out comes the {\c&H00A1ED&}crossing rate{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:54.24,0:21:57.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Shrink the strip to nothing,\Nand for well-behaved
Dialogue: 0,0:21:57.79,0:22:01.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}systems the recipe is\Nexact: Rice's formula.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:01.27,0:22:04.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The catch: few photos land\Nin a thin strip. Here,
Dialogue: 0,0:22:04.81,0:22:07.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}about two dozen out of eight thousand.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:09.05,0:22:10.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now test it against the truth.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:11.06,0:22:12.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At forty-one settings of {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&},
Dialogue: 0,0:22:13.05,0:22:16.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}long simulations count the\Ncrossings as they happen.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:16.69,0:22:21.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Rice gets only the true snapshots,\Nshuffled, plus the equations.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:21.87,0:22:24.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The two curves almost coincide.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:24.09,0:22:26.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Shade the gap,\Nand divide by the area under
Dialogue: 0,0:22:27.04,0:22:30.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the counted curve: the {\c&H00A1ED&}cumulative error{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:30.09,0:22:33.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Four percent near the threshold,\Nsix percent above it.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:33.95,0:22:36.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A good sign, on three chaotic dials that
Dialogue: 0,0:22:36.70,0:22:39.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}are not even guaranteed\Nto be well-behaved.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:41.55,0:22:44.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One caution: Rice counts\Ncrossings, not reversals.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:45.04,0:22:49.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}An excursion across zero and back\Nadds two crossings, but no reversal.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:49.93,0:22:52.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}To isolate reversals, the PhD plan counts
Dialogue: 0,0:22:52.68,0:22:55.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}crossings of a far level,\Non the other side.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:57.35,0:23:00.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So Rice needs only\Nsnapshots and the equations,
Dialogue: 0,0:23:00.71,0:23:03.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}but plenty of snapshots, and cheap ones.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:03.86,0:23:08.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Yet real dynamo simulations exist\Nat only a few {\c&H7AAF1B&}knob settings{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:08.45,0:23:11.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Between the dots,\Nthere is no shoebox at all.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:11.78,0:23:15.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}How do you get snapshots at\Na setting nobody simulated?
Dialogue: 0,0:23:17.55,0:23:20.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For VKS, which dipole should Rice count,
Dialogue: 0,0:23:20.28,0:23:24.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and what threshold turns a\Ncrossing into a reversal?
Dialogue: 0,0:23:29.52,0:23:34.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}New snapshots start as pure noise:\Na round cloud of random points.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:34.42,0:23:38.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Over it, the crowd we want:\None point per snapshot. Here,
Dialogue: 0,0:23:38.53,0:23:41.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}two mirror blobs, like the two whirlpools.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:42.75,0:23:45.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now let a {\c&HF09CB4&}wind{\c&HF7F4F2&} blow through the cloud.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:45.77,0:23:50.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each point drifts, bends,\Nand lands inside the shape of the data.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:50.95,0:23:55.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Fresh noise in, fresh snapshots\Nout, as many as you like.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:56.39,0:24:01.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}That wind is a {\c&HF09CB4&}generative model{\c&HF7F4F2&}:\Nnew, plausible examples, on demand.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:01.23,0:24:03.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The whole game is learning the wind.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:04.99,0:24:07.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One warning before the formulas.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:07.50,0:24:12.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The wind runs on its own\N{\c&HF09CB4&}transport clock{\c&HF7F4F2&}, from zero to one.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:12.13,0:24:17.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Not physical time: at the end,\Nyou hold one snapshot, not a movie.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:18.22,0:24:24.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And the {\c&HF09CB4&}wind's arrows{\c&HF7F4F2&} move snapshots, not\Nfluid: they are not the fluid's velocity.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:25.15,0:24:28.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}How is the wind learned?\NWith a simple trick.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:28.39,0:24:34.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Keep one coordinate of each point, and\Nlay the transport clock out to the right.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:34.13,0:24:37.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Pair each noise point\Nwith a random snapshot;
Dialogue: 0,0:24:37.23,0:24:39.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}join them with a straight string.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:39.81,0:24:42.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Thousands of strings, crossing everywhere.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:43.72,0:24:45.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Pick one place, at one moment.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:45.63,0:24:48.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Several strings pass through,\Npointing different ways.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:49.07,0:24:52.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The network learns one arrow\Nthere: their {\c&HF09CB4&}average direction{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:52.89,0:24:55.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}That is the whole training:\Nplain regression,
Dialogue: 0,0:24:55.62,0:24:58.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}like fitting a curve through points.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:59.01,0:25:01.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Follow the averaged wind, not the strings.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:02.00,0:25:06.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The paths bend, and drawn against\Nthe clock, they never cross.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:06.41,0:25:11.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Learn the average exactly, and the\Nnoise lands exactly on the data's crowd.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:12.95,0:25:14.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now give the wind a {\c&H7AAF1B&}knob{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:14.84,0:25:19.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Turn it: the target changes\Nshape, and the {\c&HF09CB4&}cloud{\c&HF7F4F2&} follows.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:19.14,0:25:21.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One network, every setting.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:22.07,0:25:26.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On the three dials, a real network\Ntrained at the filled dots.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:26.69,0:25:30.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It was tested at the open dots,\Nnever seen in training.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:30.79,0:25:35.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In between, no theorem promises\Nsuccess: only tests can tell.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:35.34,0:25:38.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Beyond the range, nothing was tested.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:39.00,0:25:44.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At an open dot, the network's {\c&HF09CB4&}snapshots{\c&HF7F4F2&}\Njoin the shoebox, beside simulated ones.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:45.55,0:25:49.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A generated snapshot has no\Ntrue twin, so we compare crowds.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:50.05,0:25:52.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Picture each crowd as a pile of sand.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:52.82,0:25:57.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A {\c&H00A1ED&}distribution distance{\c&HF7F4F2&} scores\Nhow different the two piles are.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:57.54,0:26:02.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The earth-mover kind counts how\Nmuch sand must move, and how far.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:03.30,0:26:07.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A perfect model, scored against\Nthe data: will it get zero?
Dialogue: 0,0:26:11.22,0:26:15.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No. Roll a fair die a thousand\Ntimes, then a thousand more.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:15.68,0:26:19.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Same die, same truth,\Nyet the two histograms differ.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:19.60,0:26:24.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}That gap is the {\c&H00A1ED&}noise floor{\c&HF7F4F2&},\Nabout what a perfect model scores.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:24.36,0:26:29.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Judge every model against it,\Nat equal sample size, never against zero.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:30.62,0:26:33.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One catch: the samples\Nmust be independent.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:33.78,0:26:38.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Overlap them, and the floor drops\Ntoo low, as the report warns.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:39.72,0:26:42.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which statistics must the\Ngenerated fields match,
Dialogue: 0,0:26:43.00,0:26:46.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and how close to the noise\Nfloor counts as success?
Dialogue: 0,0:26:51.92,0:26:55.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Physics hands the machine\Ntwo kinds of laws.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:55.11,0:26:57.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Some hold for every single snapshot.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:57.72,0:26:59.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Others hold only for the crowd.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:59.97,0:27:01.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Two shelves, still empty.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:02.68,0:27:07.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Back to the three dials, at a\N{\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&} never seen in training.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:07.14,0:27:08.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Grey: true snapshots.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:08.73,0:27:10.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Violet: a plain {\c&HF09CB4&}generator{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:10.70,0:27:12.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The shape is right.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:13.04,0:27:14.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now weigh the sides.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:14.58,0:27:17.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The truth is balanced; the machine is not.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:17.82,0:27:21.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Only about four in ten of\Nits snapshots point up.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:21.60,0:27:24.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It copied its data: below the threshold,
Dialogue: 0,0:27:24.60,0:27:29.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}each run stays in one whirlpool,\Nand the runs split unevenly.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:30.69,0:27:32.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Two ways to teach the mirror.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:32.62,0:27:34.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The first student, data augmentation,
Dialogue: 0,0:27:35.00,0:27:38.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}sees each training snapshot\Nflipped half the time.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:38.50,0:27:42.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The second, equivariance,\Nhas the mirror built into its wind:
Dialogue: 0,0:27:42.46,0:27:46.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}a mirrored noise point always\Nlands on the mirrored snapshot.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:47.61,0:27:51.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At a {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&} neither has seen,\Nwho stays balanced?
Dialogue: 0,0:27:55.21,0:27:56.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The built-in mirror.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:56.66,0:27:58.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The first student tilts a little.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:59.05,0:28:03.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Yet in the mirror's crowd,\Neach snapshot still points one way.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:03.53,0:28:06.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The mirror balances the\Ncrowd, not each snapshot.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:07.07,0:28:10.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Its card goes on the crowd\Nshelf: built in, exactly.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:12.02,0:28:14.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Here is that tilt, setting by setting.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:14.71,0:28:17.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Below the threshold,\Nruns often split unevenly:
Dialogue: 0,0:28:17.93,0:28:22.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}at zero point eight six, only\Nfive of sixteen runs point up.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:22.34,0:28:27.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The plain {\c&HF09CB4&}machine{\c&HF7F4F2&} copies the imbalance,\Nand spills it onto unseen settings.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:28.54,0:28:32.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Augmentation sits on the {\c&H00A1ED&}noise floor{\c&HF7F4F2&}\Nat its training settings.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:32.76,0:28:36.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Between them, it drifts:\Nthe network fills the gaps freely.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:36.79,0:28:40.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The built-in mirror stays\Non the floor everywhere.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:40.20,0:28:44.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Flipped copies teach the mirror\Nonly where there are data.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:45.35,0:28:48.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Can the energy budget catch the tilt?
Dialogue: 0,0:28:48.26,0:28:51.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No. Each whirlpool\Nbalances its own budget.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:51.64,0:28:55.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So a mix of the two passes,\Nin any proportion.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:55.25,0:29:00.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On a check of crowd laws, the most\Nlopsided machine even edges ahead.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:00.67,0:29:03.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The mirror needs its own tool.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:04.22,0:29:06.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Remember the budget of the shoebox.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:06.70,0:29:09.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Snapshot by snapshot,\Nenergy rises or falls.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:09.81,0:29:14.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Only the crowd balances, on average,\Nlike a bank account over the years.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:15.81,0:29:18.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now, a tempting fix for the machine.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:18.25,0:29:23.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Force every generated snapshot to balance\Nits budget exactly. Better, or worse?
Dialogue: 0,0:29:27.51,0:29:28.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Worse.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:27.95,0:29:31.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Push each snapshot until\Nits energy stops changing:
Dialogue: 0,0:29:31.52,0:29:34.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the wide pile collapses\Ninto a spike at zero.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:34.97,0:29:37.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A ledger where every single day balances.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:37.94,0:29:40.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No real account looks like that.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:41.15,0:29:45.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The {\c&H00A1ED&}distribution distance{\c&HF7F4F2&}\Nto the truth grows ninefold.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:45.29,0:29:48.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A short proof says why:\Neven a perfect generator,
Dialogue: 0,0:29:48.91,0:29:52.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}forced this way, cannot match the truth.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:52.17,0:29:55.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So the budget goes on the crowd shelf.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:55.08,0:30:00.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On the next test bench, a cruder\Nscore is fooled by such a spike.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:01.25,0:30:04.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Impose the budget on the\Ncrowd, during training.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:04.62,0:30:08.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Draw a batch of snapshots at\Nany {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&}, even without data.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:08.68,0:30:12.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Average their energy change\Nand square it: this penalty,
Dialogue: 0,0:30:12.49,0:30:16.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the crowd loss, vanishes\Nonly when the crowd balances.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:17.36,0:30:18.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But there is a trap.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:18.72,0:30:22.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Even for a balanced crowd,\Na batch's average wobbles;
Dialogue: 0,0:30:22.20,0:30:24.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}its square grows with the scatter.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:24.73,0:30:27.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So training also squeezes the scatter,
Dialogue: 0,0:30:27.22,0:30:31.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}nudging every snapshot toward\Nbalance: toward the spike.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:31.21,0:30:34.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It showed up while\Nbuilding this experiment.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:35.11,0:30:38.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The fix: split the batch\Ninto two independent
Dialogue: 0,0:30:38.14,0:30:40.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}juries and multiply their averages.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:40.80,0:30:45.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On average, the scatter drops out,\Nleaving only the crowd's imbalance.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:45.72,0:30:48.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Does it pay off? The next tests will tell.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:50.15,0:30:53.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In three dimensions,\Nwhich budgets should generated
Dialogue: 0,0:30:53.34,0:30:57.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}fields meet on average,\Nand must their spread match too?
Dialogue: 0,0:31:02.55,0:31:05.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}New test bench: whole\Nfields, not three dials.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:05.79,0:31:09.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A flat square of conducting fluid,\Nits edges wrapping around.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:10.07,0:31:13.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In blue, the swirl of\Nthe {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&}; in vermillion,
Dialogue: 0,0:31:13.18,0:31:16.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the electric currents behind the {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:17.02,0:31:20.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One knob, {\c&H7AAF1B&}eta{\c&HF7F4F2&}: how fast the field smudges.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:20.28,0:31:23.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But a flat world cannot sustain a dynamo.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:23.47,0:31:26.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Here a random forcing\Nkeeps the field alive:
Dialogue: 0,0:31:26.78,0:31:30.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}a training ground for laws, not a dynamo.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:31.35,0:31:35.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Every training field passes the\Nbox test: it is divergence-free.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:35.74,0:31:37.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Train a plain {\c&HF09CB4&}generator{\c&HF7F4F2&} on them.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:38.83,0:31:40.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Will its fields be divergence-free?
Dialogue: 0,0:31:44.78,0:31:45.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:45.01,0:31:48.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now lines appear and\Nvanish inside the box.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:48.26,0:31:51.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Relative to the field's own variations,
Dialogue: 0,0:31:51.11,0:31:55.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the divergence is zero point\Nfour one: a defect of order one.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:56.78,0:32:00.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A soft penalty only lowers\Nit to zero point three
Dialogue: 0,0:32:00.59,0:32:04.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}four for the {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&}, zero\Npoint one six for the field.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:04.93,0:32:10.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And the magnetic energy comes out\Ntwo point seven times too high.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:10.12,0:32:14.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}This law belongs on the\Nshelf for every snapshot.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:15.25,0:32:18.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}First fix: draw a hill map, not arrows,
Dialogue: 0,0:32:18.16,0:32:21.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and let the {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&} run\Nalong its contour lines.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:21.81,0:32:24.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Where the lines crowd, it runs faster,
Dialogue: 0,0:32:24.65,0:32:28.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}so nothing piles up or leaks,\Nwhatever the landscape.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:28.91,0:32:31.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The box agrees: lines in, lines out.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:32.59,0:32:36.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So the {\c&HF09CB4&}machine{\c&HF7F4F2&} draws two hill maps,\Nfor the flow and the field:
Dialogue: 0,0:32:37.03,0:32:41.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}zero divergence by construction,\Nhowever badly it is trained.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:42.53,0:32:48.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Second fix, for any field: split it into\Nswirl plus slope, and delete the slope.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:48.68,0:32:54.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Wave by wave, remove the part of each\Narrow along the wave's own direction.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:55.35,0:33:00.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The gauges fall to rounding error,\Ndown to ten to the minus seventeen.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:00.44,0:33:04.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Measured, though, with the very\Noperator used to enforce it.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:05.99,0:33:11.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Back to the plain {\c&HF09CB4&}generator{\c&HF7F4F2&}:\Nwhy does it miss, trained on clean data?
Dialogue: 0,0:33:11.49,0:33:14.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Follow the part of a\Nfield breaking the law.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:14.99,0:33:19.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}To bring it exactly to zero,\Nthe {\c&HF09CB4&}wind{\c&HF7F4F2&} must act as a spring
Dialogue: 0,0:33:19.51,0:33:24.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}that turns infinitely stiff as\Nthe transport clock runs out.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:25.38,0:33:31.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A bounded network cannot, and a sampler\Nwith finite steps cannot follow either.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:31.17,0:33:34.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A gap of order one remains:\Nalmost is not exactly.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:36.02,0:33:40.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now the crowd law: on average,\Nenergy in equals energy out.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:40.70,0:33:45.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three ways to impose it,\Nat settings of {\c&H7AAF1B&}eta{\c&HF7F4F2&} never seen.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:45.96,0:33:49.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One: during training, the crowd\Nloss, with its two juries.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:49.97,0:33:52.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Against a control run of equal length,
Dialogue: 0,0:33:52.52,0:33:57.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the {\c&H00A1ED&}distribution distance{\c&HF7F4F2&} drops from\Ntwo point five to zero point seven.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:57.65,0:33:59.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The control itself gains nothing.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:00.83,0:34:04.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Two: after training,\Nreweight the generated crowd.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:04.47,0:34:07.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Where the model is worst,\Na poll of five hundred
Dialogue: 0,0:34:07.84,0:34:11.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}shrinks to one person,\Nand the crowd gets worse.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:12.42,0:34:15.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three: rescale every\Nsnapshot onto the budget.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:15.63,0:34:18.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each snapshot now dissipates\Nthe same: a spike,
Dialogue: 0,0:34:18.78,0:34:21.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}under a thousandth of the true spread.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:21.62,0:34:26.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Yet the same {\c&H00A1ED&}distribution distance{\c&HF7F4F2&},\Nbuilt on spectra, improves: it is fooled.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:26.99,0:34:31.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Only the spread gives the spike away.\NThe crowd loss shows no spike.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:32.63,0:34:35.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One training seed each,\Ninterpolation only,
Dialogue: 0,0:34:35.31,0:34:38.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}every model far above the noise floor.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:39.19,0:34:41.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Back to the two shelves, now full.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:41.66,0:34:45.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For every snapshot: zero\Ndivergence. Build it in,
Dialogue: 0,0:34:45.13,0:34:49.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}or project, and it holds exactly;\Na penalty only roughly.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:50.36,0:34:54.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For the crowd: the energy budget.\NImpose it on the crowd
Dialogue: 0,0:34:54.19,0:34:58.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}during training. Reweighting afterwards\Nfails where the model is poor;
Dialogue: 0,0:34:58.97,0:35:02.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}forcing it onto each\Nsnapshot makes a spike.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:03.18,0:35:08.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And the mirror? Each snapshot is\Nlopsided, so it is a crowd law,
Dialogue: 0,0:35:08.31,0:35:12.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}yet a network can carry\Nit exactly, built in.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:13.12,0:35:18.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One rule sorts them all:\Nenforce each law where it is true.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:21.15,0:35:25.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Put the pieces together:\Nthe machine this study proposes.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:25.33,0:35:30.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A {\c&HF09CB4&}wind{\c&HF7F4F2&} carries noise to a symmetric\Ncrowd of codes, mirror built in.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:30.30,0:35:35.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A decoder turns each code into hill maps,\Nso every field is divergence-free.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:35.87,0:35:39.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And the energy budget\Nis imposed on the crowd.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:40.14,0:35:44.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It has not been built as a whole:\Nthe experiments tested pieces,
Dialogue: 0,0:35:44.82,0:35:50.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}on three dials and a flat sheet, with\Nsmall networks on an ordinary processor.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:51.72,0:35:56.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One question remains: from snapshots,\Nhow well can we answer {\c&H00A1ED&}how often{\c&HF7F4F2&}?
Dialogue: 0,0:35:57.95,0:36:01.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In the partners' solver snapshots,\Nwhich laws hold exactly,
Dialogue: 0,0:36:01.41,0:36:03.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and under which discrete operator?
Dialogue: 0,0:36:08.92,0:36:12.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The toll counter is back:\Nevery car is now {\c&HF09CB4&}generated{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:12.67,0:36:16.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}They come from the machine\Nwith the built-in mirror:
Dialogue: 0,0:36:16.28,0:36:18.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}twenty thousand at each of forty-one
Dialogue: 0,0:36:18.79,0:36:22.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}settings of {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&},\Nmost unseen in training.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:22.08,0:36:26.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Rice, with the equations,\Nturns them into a {\c&H00A1ED&}crossing rate{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:27.08,0:36:29.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Against the counted truth,\Nfar from the threshold,
Dialogue: 0,0:36:30.02,0:36:32.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the {\c&H00A1ED&}cumulative error{\c&HF7F4F2&} is fourteen percent.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:32.74,0:36:35.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Setting by setting, the miss\Naverages seventeen percent,
Dialogue: 0,0:36:36.05,0:36:39.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and reaches sixty-five\Npercent at two settings.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:39.13,0:36:42.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Near the threshold, the error\Nclimbs to forty-two percent.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:43.60,0:36:45.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One model, one training seed.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:45.74,0:36:50.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Rice needs the equations,\Nand gives a rate, not waiting times.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:50.33,0:36:54.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So, the study's third question:\Ncan a machine tell us {\c&H00A1ED&}how often{\c&HF7F4F2&}?
Dialogue: 0,0:36:55.06,0:37:01.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For crossings on three dials: far from the\Nthreshold, roughly yes; near it, not yet.
Dialogue: 0,0:37:02.85,0:37:07.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Zoom in. The truth is silent\Nup to zero point eight six.
Dialogue: 0,0:37:07.23,0:37:10.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But the machine changes\Nsmoothly with {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:37:11.56,0:37:16.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Will its crossing rate switch\Non early, late, or on time?
Dialogue: 0,0:37:19.98,0:37:22.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Early and late, but never on time.
Dialogue: 0,0:37:22.58,0:37:25.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Below the threshold,\Nit leaks false crossings:
Dialogue: 0,0:37:25.98,0:37:28.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}up to a tenth of the rate just above.
Dialogue: 0,0:37:29.01,0:37:35.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At zero point eight six five, it is late:\Nless than a sixth of the counted rate.
Dialogue: 0,0:37:35.13,0:37:38.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Just above, it overshoots:\Nfive crossings per
Dialogue: 0,0:37:38.47,0:37:42.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}thousand time units,\Nagainst three point four.
Dialogue: 0,0:37:43.39,0:37:46.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Why? Picture the machine as a stiff sheet:
Dialogue: 0,0:37:46.47,0:37:50.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}as {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&} turns, its crowd\Nchanges only so fast.
Dialogue: 0,0:37:50.23,0:37:56.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The truth climbs a steep ramp: just above\Nzero point eight six, the corridor opens.
Dialogue: 0,0:37:57.44,0:38:01.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The sheet rounds the corner:\Ntoo early, then too low.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:01.53,0:38:07.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The data show both: early strays in the\Ncorridor, then too few at the threshold.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:08.61,0:38:14.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A theorem: at a true jump, a smooth\Nmachine misses by at least half of it.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:15.05,0:38:20.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Unless it is very supple,\Nor told which state the system is in.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:21.43,0:38:27.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Here, a ramp, not a jump, and one seed:\Nconsistent with the theorem, not proof.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:27.28,0:38:30.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Nor does the theorem\Nexplain the overshoot.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:32.09,0:38:35.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The crowd loss, on three\Ndials, at unseen settings.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:35.90,0:38:39.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On the good machine,\Nit halves the crowd-law residuals,
Dialogue: 0,0:38:39.86,0:38:42.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}even for laws it never enforced.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:42.47,0:38:48.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But the spread of energy changes shrinks\Nby a quarter: a faint echo of the spike.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:49.43,0:38:52.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On a deliberately biased\Nmachine, it fails:
Dialogue: 0,0:38:52.37,0:38:55.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}one run diverged, the other got worse.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:55.27,0:38:57.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On the crossing rate, with one seed:
Dialogue: 0,0:38:57.74,0:39:01.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}slightly better near the\Nthreshold, worse far from it.
Dialogue: 0,0:39:03.35,0:39:06.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now the strictest judge: the {\c&H00A1ED&}noise floor{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:39:06.39,0:39:09.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On three dials, the best\Nmachine sits at about
Dialogue: 0,0:39:09.68,0:39:13.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}twice its floor;\Nbut three numbers are easy.
Dialogue: 0,0:39:13.14,0:39:17.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On the flat sheet of plasma,\Nevery model's spectra miss
Dialogue: 0,0:39:17.10,0:39:21.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}far more than two data samples\Ndiffer: at best, sixfold.
Dialogue: 0,0:39:22.34,0:39:28.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Two seeds on the dials, one on the sheet;\Nthe PhD plan asks for at least three.
Dialogue: 0,0:39:28.56,0:39:31.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Every test sits between trained settings.
Dialogue: 0,0:39:33.39,0:39:36.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The scoreboard: eight mechanisms,\Neach with a verdict.
Dialogue: 0,0:39:36.87,0:39:39.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For every snapshot:\Nhill maps and projection,
Dialogue: 0,0:39:39.63,0:39:41.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}exact; the penalty, approximate.
Dialogue: 0,0:39:41.90,0:39:44.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For the crowd: the mirror,\Nexact; the crowd loss,
Dialogue: 0,0:39:44.93,0:39:47.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}useful but not clean; the rest, failures.
Dialogue: 0,0:39:48.67,0:39:51.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Back to the study's three questions.
Dialogue: 0,0:39:51.30,0:39:54.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One: between the dots,\Nthe shapes come out right,
Dialogue: 0,0:39:54.75,0:39:57.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}but no model reaches its {\c&H00A1ED&}noise floor{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:39:57.66,0:40:02.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Two: each law where it holds,\Nexactly yes, {\c&H00A1ED&}on average{\c&HF7F4F2&} partly.
Dialogue: 0,0:40:02.12,0:40:07.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three, {\c&H00A1ED&}how often{\c&HF7F4F2&}: roughly right far\Nfrom the threshold, poor near it.
Dialogue: 0,0:40:08.06,0:40:08.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three lessons.
Dialogue: 0,0:40:09.06,0:40:10.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Exact laws: build them in.
Dialogue: 0,0:40:10.92,0:40:15.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Crowd laws: impose them on the\Ncrowd, never on each snapshot.
Dialogue: 0,0:40:15.27,0:40:20.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And judge every result against the\Nnoise floor, with enough seeds.
Dialogue: 0,0:40:21.99,0:40:26.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which settings should get runs\Nlong enough to count reversals,
Dialogue: 0,0:40:26.26,0:40:29.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}so a predicted rate can be checked?
Dialogue: 0,0:40:34.19,0:40:37.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So far, every test ran\Non toy models. Before
Dialogue: 0,0:40:37.55,0:40:41.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}real dynamos, back to\Nthe map of the field.
Dialogue: 0,0:40:41.13,0:40:46.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Zoom into its thin data-driven\Nlane: it opens into four tracks.
Dialogue: 0,0:40:47.01,0:40:51.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A laboratory dynamo sets every\Ntrack four tests: three dimensions,
Dialogue: 0,0:40:51.42,0:40:56.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}walls with iron and a vacuum, a tiny\N{\c&H7AAF1B&}magnetic Prandtl number{\c&HF7F4F2&}, and rare flips.
Dialogue: 0,0:40:58.45,0:41:02.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}First track: fast surrogates,\Nnetworks trained to imitate a solver.
Dialogue: 0,0:41:02.72,0:41:07.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Some carry the equations in their loss;\Nothers learn maps between whole fields.
Dialogue: 0,0:41:07.74,0:41:12.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For conducting fluids, they now reach\Nflat, two-dimensional turbulence.
Dialogue: 0,0:41:12.26,0:41:15.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For the Earth, recall the\Ntwenty twenty-two study:
Dialogue: 0,0:41:15.33,0:41:19.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the methods it tried could\Nnot reliably foresee a flip.
Dialogue: 0,0:41:19.90,0:41:23.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Their weak spot: trained to\Nminimize a mean-square error,
Dialogue: 0,0:41:23.75,0:41:26.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}ensembles of them\Nregress to the mean flow.
Dialogue: 0,0:41:26.95,0:41:30.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A cloud with two polarities\Ncollapses to its middle.
Dialogue: 0,0:41:31.79,0:41:33.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Second track: {\c&HF09CB4&}generative models{\c&HF7F4F2&},
Dialogue: 0,0:41:33.54,0:41:36.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}which learn the whole cloud,\Nnot just its middle.
Dialogue: 0,0:41:36.54,0:41:38.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Adversarial networks\Nfirst, then diffusion,
Dialogue: 0,0:41:38.91,0:41:41.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}then flow matching,\Nthe family the study used.
Dialogue: 0,0:41:41.75,0:41:45.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}They now dream three-dimensional\Nturbulence, global weather ensembles,
Dialogue: 0,0:41:45.66,0:41:48.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and steady turbulent\Nstates for chosen {\c&H7AAF1B&}settings{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:41:49.54,0:41:52.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Trained on dynamo\Nsimulations? We found none.
Dialogue: 0,0:41:54.42,0:41:58.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Third track: making samples\Nobey the laws. Four families.
Dialogue: 0,0:41:58.33,0:42:02.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Hard: build the law into the network,\Nsay a potential whose curl
Dialogue: 0,0:42:02.61,0:42:06.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}has no divergence, or project\Neach sample while drawing it.
Dialogue: 0,0:42:06.85,0:42:10.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Soft: a penalty in the loss,\Nobeyed only approximately.
Dialogue: 0,0:42:10.63,0:42:13.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On the {\c&H00A1ED&}distribution{\c&HF7F4F2&}: constrain the crowd,
Dialogue: 0,0:42:13.35,0:42:17.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}by its covariance, or by the\Nequation a steady cloud obeys.
Dialogue: 0,0:42:17.56,0:42:21.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And symmetry: an equivariant\Nflow gives a symmetric cloud.
Dialogue: 0,0:42:22.84,0:42:26.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A twenty twenty-six preprint\Ncompared ways to impose zero
Dialogue: 0,0:42:26.14,0:42:29.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}divergence on flat flows:\Nno single strategy dominates.
Dialogue: 0,0:42:29.62,0:42:33.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In three dimensions, we found two\Nexactly divergence-free generators,
Dialogue: 0,0:42:33.68,0:42:35.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}both preprints: periodic-box turbulence,
Dialogue: 0,0:42:36.03,0:42:38.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and a current-free field\Nin the Sun's corona.
Dialogue: 0,0:42:38.98,0:42:42.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}None handles walls, a vacuum\Noutside and iron inside.
Dialogue: 0,0:42:43.35,0:42:47.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Fourth track: data assimilation,\Nmerging a model with a few measurements.
Dialogue: 0,0:42:47.84,0:42:52.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Learned priors came in through compressed\Nlatent spaces, then diffusion models.
Dialogue: 0,0:42:52.70,0:42:57.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And a library now plugs deep networks into\NKalman filters and variational methods.
Dialogue: 0,0:42:58.64,0:43:03.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}We found no diffusion prior used\Nthis way for conducting fluids.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:03.46,0:43:06.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Near a flip, the answer\Nhas two polarities,
Dialogue: 0,0:43:06.61,0:43:11.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and a prior that ignores the\Nmirror could bias it toward one.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:12.59,0:43:15.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now the rings: papers\Nco-signed by the supervisors.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:15.70,0:43:20.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Dynamo side: Taylor–Green dynamos pushed\Nto small {\c&H7AAF1B&}magnetic Prandtl numbers{\c&HF7F4F2&},
Dialogue: 0,0:43:20.16,0:43:24.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and the VKS tank with its iron\Nimpellers, simulated in two codes.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:24.32,0:43:27.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In twenty twenty-six, both\Ncodes produced VKS reversals,
Dialogue: 0,0:43:27.62,0:43:30.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and a four-dial model\Nreproduces their main features.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:31.04,0:43:34.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Learning side: autoencoder\Nlatent spaces read through
Dialogue: 0,0:43:34.15,0:43:37.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}classical flow modes,\Nvon Kármán flow included.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:37.21,0:43:39.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And assimilation and\Ndiffusion models built
Dialogue: 0,0:43:39.72,0:43:42.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}on deep networks,\Noutside conducting fluids.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:43.89,0:43:47.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three ingredients are in hand.\NBut in what we found,
Dialogue: 0,0:43:47.69,0:43:53.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}none of the four has published a\N{\c&HF09CB4&}generative model{\c&HF7F4F2&} trained on dynamo data.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:54.55,0:43:57.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Where the {\c&HF09CB4&}generative{\c&HF7F4F2&} and\Nconstraint tracks cross
Dialogue: 0,0:43:57.61,0:44:00.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the dynamo tracks, we found the map empty.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:00.59,0:44:03.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}An independent thesis\Nproposal aims at that spot:
Dialogue: 0,0:44:03.71,0:44:06.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}five gaps, each beside\Nits nearest neighbour.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:07.78,0:44:09.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One: a generator of dynamo fields.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:10.07,0:44:12.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Two: {\c&HE58739&}velocity{\c&HF7F4F2&} and\Nmagnetic field with exactly
Dialogue: 0,0:44:12.99,0:44:17.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}zero divergence, in a walled\Ncylinder with iron and vacuum.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:17.12,0:44:20.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three: energy budgets\Nimposed on the {\c&H00A1ED&}distribution{\c&HF7F4F2&},
Dialogue: 0,0:44:20.36,0:44:23.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}with no target value,\Neven where there are no data.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:23.96,0:44:27.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Four: the flip rate read from\Nsnapshots with Rice's formula.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:27.99,0:44:31.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Five: one generator that\Ncrosses the dynamo threshold.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:32.92,0:44:37.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The study tested pieces on toy cases:\Nexact potentials on a flat sheet,
Dialogue: 0,0:44:37.84,0:44:42.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}a budget imposed without data,\NRice's formula on three dials.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:43.55,0:44:48.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The field moves fast: a twenty twenty-six\Nconference paper claims to be the
Dialogue: 0,0:44:48.45,0:44:53.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}first to recognize a trade-off between\Nphysical and {\c&H00A1ED&}distributional{\c&HF7F4F2&} accuracy.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:53.70,0:44:56.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The study's lesson,\Nnever force a crowd law
Dialogue: 0,0:44:56.46,0:44:59.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}on each snapshot,\Nmust be placed against it.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:00.48,0:45:03.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Not claimed: waiting times between flips,
Dialogue: 0,0:45:03.15,0:45:08.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}sodium's tiny {\c&H7AAF1B&}magnetic Prandtl number{\c&HF7F4F2&},\Nthe Earth's core, real laboratory data.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:08.54,0:45:11.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each empty spot is a\Nsearch result, not a proof,
Dialogue: 0,0:45:11.64,0:45:14.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}to be checked again before every paper.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:16.15,0:45:19.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}First VKS data: should\Nthe generator train on the
Dialogue: 0,0:45:19.50,0:45:23.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}LATU periodic grid,\Nor the SFEMaNS cylinder mesh?
Dialogue: 0,0:45:28.35,0:45:29.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Back to the staircase.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:30.01,0:45:33.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The first two steps are\Nlit by small experiments:
Dialogue: 0,0:45:33.56,0:45:36.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}three numbers, then a flat sheet.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:36.25,0:45:39.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The third, the twisting\Nbox, has only a preview.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:40.76,0:45:43.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}From there up, every step is a bet,
Dialogue: 0,0:45:43.34,0:45:48.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}written down before the data exist,\Nin an independent PhD proposal.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:49.75,0:45:54.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}To climb, the study first\Nbuilt a factory for the data.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:54.10,0:45:56.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A recipe card sets the {\c&H7AAF1B&}knobs{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:56.39,0:46:01.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Ovens run one simulation per setting,\Non a laptop or a cluster.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:01.37,0:46:04.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each result is sealed in a labelled jar:
Dialogue: 0,0:46:04.43,0:46:08.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}a unique number,\Nthe code version, a checksum.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:09.15,0:46:14.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A ledger records every jar, a menu\Nsplits them for training and testing,
Dialogue: 0,0:46:14.39,0:46:18.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the {\c&HF09CB4&}chef{\c&HF7F4F2&} learns, and a\Ncritic scores the result.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:19.09,0:46:22.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It all runs end to end\Non an ordinary processor,
Dialogue: 0,0:46:23.03,0:46:27.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}never yet on a graphics\Ncard or a real cluster.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:27.19,0:46:31.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A grey tag names the file\Neach empty frame awaits.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:32.62,0:46:35.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}First product off the\Nline: the flat sheet,
Dialogue: 0,0:46:35.36,0:46:38.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}eleven {\c&H7AAF1B&}diffusivities{\c&HF7F4F2&}, sixteen runs each.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:38.20,0:46:41.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The bet, a check on the\Ndata: at every setting,
Dialogue: 0,0:46:41.24,0:46:46.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the {\c&H00A1ED&}average dissipation{\c&HF7F4F2&} matches the\Ninjected power, within five percent.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:47.59,0:46:49.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Bet one: the twisting box.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:49.38,0:46:53.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At thirty-two cubed, the small\Nsweep matched the published windows,
Dialogue: 0,0:46:53.88,0:46:58.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}but near seventeen and a half the\Nsign of growth stayed unresolved.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:59.58,0:47:05.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The campaign doubles the resolution and\Nsteps {\c&H7AAF1B&}Rm{\c&HF7F4F2&} by whole numbers up to forty.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:05.24,0:47:08.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The bet: growth still\Npositive at seventeen,
Dialogue: 0,0:47:08.39,0:47:12.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}already negative at eighteen,\Nwith two fits agreeing:
Dialogue: 0,0:47:12.18,0:47:16.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}one over the kept part of the run,\None over its last quarter.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:18.54,0:47:21.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Confidence: medium. At eighteen,\Nthe small sweep's two
Dialogue: 0,0:47:21.94,0:47:25.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}fits already differed by\Nmore than the rate itself.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:25.45,0:47:30.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Worth asking before the run: add three\Npoints between seventeen and eighteen?
Dialogue: 0,0:47:31.85,0:47:33.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Bet two, the first true dynamo:
Dialogue: 0,0:47:34.01,0:47:37.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}a Taylor-Green flow of\Ncounter-rotating eddies.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:37.57,0:47:40.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now the {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} pushes back on the flow.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:40.32,0:47:44.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Above a threshold, a tiny {\c&H3468EB&}seed{\c&HF7F4F2&}\Nshould grow, then level off.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:45.49,0:47:50.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Twelve runs cross four Reynolds\Nnumbers with three values of {\c&H7AAF1B&}Pm{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:50.47,0:47:55.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The bet: a threshold in {\c&H7AAF1B&}Rm{\c&HF7F4F2&},\Nbracketed for at least two values of Pm.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:57.47,0:48:02.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Confidence: medium. Published runs\Ndefine the Reynolds number differently,
Dialogue: 0,0:48:02.30,0:48:05.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}so the threshold may fall off this grid.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:05.21,0:48:09.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Near threshold, a run may still be\Ngrowing when its snapshots are kept.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:09.99,0:48:13.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Lengthen those runs,\Nor flag and exclude them?
Dialogue: 0,0:48:14.49,0:48:16.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Some things need no bet.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:16.08,0:48:19.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In three dimensions,\Nhill maps become potentials:
Dialogue: 0,0:48:19.23,0:48:22.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the {\c&HF09CB4&}generator{\c&HF7F4F2&} draws them,\Nthen takes their curl.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:22.60,0:48:26.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So the {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&} and the field stay\Ndivergence-free to rounding,
Dialogue: 0,0:48:26.37,0:48:28.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}even with untrained weights.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:29.36,0:48:34.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Mirror the starting noise, and the\Nsample comes out with its {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} flipped.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:34.63,0:48:37.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}That shows the mechanism, not the quality.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:38.79,0:48:41.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Everything else must be measured.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:41.20,0:48:45.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}First test: does the {\c&HF09CB4&}generator{\c&HF7F4F2&}\Nget the energy spectra right,
Dialogue: 0,0:48:45.46,0:48:49.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}within a tenth of a decade,\Nat the settings it trained on?
Dialogue: 0,0:48:50.78,0:48:51.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Confidence: low.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:51.90,0:48:55.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In two dimensions, the best\Nmodels only just got there,
Dialogue: 0,0:48:55.65,0:49:01.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and here each run keeps forty snapshots,\Nagainst five thousand in the plan.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:03.15,0:49:07.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Leave the periodic box for a real\Nvessel, and exact gets harder.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:08.02,0:49:11.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The potential needs a\Ngauge and a wall condition;
Dialogue: 0,0:49:11.61,0:49:14.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}then each {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} has exactly one.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:15.15,0:49:20.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At the soft-iron impellers, {\c&H3468EB&}field lines{\c&HF7F4F2&}\Nbend sharply at the iron's surface,
Dialogue: 0,0:49:20.42,0:49:25.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}so a smooth potential cannot be\Nexact: it needs a kink there.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:26.41,0:49:31.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Outside, the {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} must match a\Ncurrent-free field in the vacuum.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:31.30,0:49:33.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The rule is linear but nonlocal:
Dialogue: 0,0:49:33.63,0:49:38.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} at one point of the\Nwall depends on the whole wall.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:38.37,0:49:43.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Imposing it exactly and cheaply,\Nfor every sample, is still open.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:44.45,0:49:47.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The top steps are\Nsimulations of real machines:
Dialogue: 0,0:49:47.40,0:49:51.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the VKS cylinder, and DRESDYN,\Na precessing cylinder.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:51.93,0:49:53.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Their bets are already written.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:54.00,0:49:57.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For VKS: the four main patterns\Nwith the right energy shares,
Dialogue: 0,0:49:57.96,0:50:02.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and Rice's reversal rate inside\Nthe simulated count's error bars.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:02.47,0:50:07.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For DRESDYN: the threshold at an unseen\Nprecession rate, within ten percent.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:08.95,0:50:11.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Those simulations belong\Nto the project's partners,
Dialogue: 0,0:50:12.07,0:50:14.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}are not public, and must be requested.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:16.39,0:50:19.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Before the Taylor-Green runs, which\Nforcing, Reynolds number definition
Dialogue: 0,0:50:20.04,0:50:23.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and resolution make their threshold\Ncomparable with published ones?
Dialogue: 0,0:50:28.99,0:50:32.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Every chapter so far\Nhas ended on a question.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:32.53,0:50:34.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The empty seats raised more.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:35.64,0:50:38.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Hang each one under the step it shapes.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:38.52,0:50:43.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Bet one: whether to add settings\Nbetween seventeen and eighteen.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:43.24,0:50:47.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Bet two: the {\c&H3468EB&}seed{\c&HF7F4F2&}, the Reynolds number,\Nthe length of each run.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:47.89,0:50:50.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The {\c&HF09CB4&}generator{\c&HF7F4F2&}: what it must match.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:50.39,0:50:52.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The top steps: the partners' data.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:53.80,0:50:57.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A staircase of bets is a\Nstaircase of decisions,
Dialogue: 0,0:50:57.49,0:51:00.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and the first ones come before any run.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:02.79,0:51:06.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}First, the runs: these answers\Nchange what gets computed.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:07.39,0:51:11.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Near a threshold, start from a\Ntiny {\c&H3468EB&}seed{\c&HF7F4F2&}, a strong one, or both?
Dialogue: 0,0:51:11.74,0:51:15.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which values of {\c&H7AAF1B&}Pm{\c&HF7F4F2&}, since\Nsodium's is out of reach?
Dialogue: 0,0:51:15.20,0:51:18.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Settings spread evenly,\Nor packed near each threshold?
Dialogue: 0,0:51:18.87,0:51:21.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}How many independent\Nsnapshots per setting?
Dialogue: 0,0:51:21.79,0:51:24.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}How long must runs near a threshold last?
Dialogue: 0,0:51:24.58,0:51:27.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which forcing, Reynolds\Nnumber and grid make the
Dialogue: 0,0:51:27.73,0:51:31.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Taylor-Green threshold\Ncomparable with published ones?
Dialogue: 0,0:51:31.58,0:51:33.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And which campaign comes first?
Dialogue: 0,0:51:34.89,0:51:40.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Then the framing: how each result will\Nbe judged, decided before the data exist.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:40.66,0:51:45.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which statistics must generated\Nfields match, down to the shape of
Dialogue: 0,0:51:45.30,0:51:50.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}their field lines, and how close to\Nthe {\c&H00A1ED&}noise floor{\c&HF7F4F2&} counts as success?
Dialogue: 0,0:51:50.53,0:51:55.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which budgets must hold {\c&H00A1ED&}on average{\c&HF7F4F2&},\Nand must their spread match too?
Dialogue: 0,0:51:56.62,0:52:00.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One answer, not about physics,\Ncould make most renting unnecessary.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:01.00,0:52:02.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which question is it?
Dialogue: 0,0:52:05.77,0:52:07.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Where the computing happens.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:07.96,0:52:12.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}If a lab has graphics cards an\Nintern can use, or there is already
Dialogue: 0,0:52:12.98,0:52:17.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}a Jean Zay project to join,\Nrenting shrinks to a short trial.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:17.93,0:52:23.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And for the top steps: which partner\Nsimulations, in what form, and by when?
Dialogue: 0,0:52:25.05,0:52:28.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The second shelf matters\Nfor the internship plan
Dialogue: 0,0:52:28.28,0:52:31.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and the data request,\Nnot for the first campaigns.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:31.93,0:52:35.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which settings get runs long\Nenough to count reversals?
Dialogue: 0,0:52:35.76,0:52:38.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which operator decides what exact means?
Dialogue: 0,0:52:38.54,0:52:41.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which labels must each snapshot carry?
Dialogue: 0,0:52:41.18,0:52:43.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And may new results appear in this film?
Dialogue: 0,0:52:44.86,0:52:50.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The last shelf can wait: the top steps'\Ndetails, and how to frame a first paper.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:52.62,0:52:57.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The first shelf fits in one message,\Nto write before renting anything.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:57.66,0:52:59.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}This ranking is only a proposal.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:59.96,0:53:02.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The supervisors may well change it.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:03.38,0:53:06.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Several answers could\Nchange what gets computed,
Dialogue: 0,0:53:06.44,0:53:09.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}or whether anything is rented at all.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:09.10,0:53:11.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The cheapest experiment\Nis a good question,
Dialogue: 0,0:53:11.83,0:53:14.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}asked before renting a graphics card.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:20.09,0:53:22.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Questions first, then computing.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:22.24,0:53:25.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Here is what those questions\Nrest on, in two columns.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:25.80,0:53:30.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On the left, what three small experiments\Nmeasured on an ordinary processor.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:30.91,0:53:33.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Toy problems, one or two training seeds,
Dialogue: 0,0:53:33.51,0:53:38.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and machines tested only between\Nthe settings they were trained on.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:39.07,0:53:45.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On the right, what the staircase bets,\Neach with the rule that will settle it.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:45.33,0:53:49.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each lamp shows a verdict only\Nonce its data file arrives,
Dialogue: 0,0:53:49.84,0:53:52.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and the film is updated with it.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:54.42,0:53:58.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Back to the compass,\Nand to the two questions.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:59.52,0:54:01.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}When will this needle turn?
Dialogue: 0,0:54:01.50,0:54:03.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For the Earth, no one can say.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:03.70,0:54:06.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In a simple computer\Nmodel, chaos makes the
Dialogue: 0,0:54:06.75,0:54:10.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}forecast fade before\Nthe average wait ends.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:10.09,0:54:16.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But {\c&H00A1ED&}how often{\c&HF7F4F2&} the field flips, counted as\Ncrossings of zero, can still be answered.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:16.19,0:54:21.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The answer hides in a shoebox of\Nsnapshots, read with the equations.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:21.18,0:54:27.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And a {\c&HF09CB4&}machine{\c&HF7F4F2&} can learn to dream such\Nsnapshots, between the simulated settings.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:28.35,0:54:31.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On small test problems, it partly works.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:31.30,0:54:34.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The mirror holds by construction,\Nat every setting.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:35.06,0:54:37.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On three dials, the {\c&H00A1ED&}rate of zero crossings{\c&HF7F4F2&}
Dialogue: 0,0:54:38.06,0:54:42.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}is roughly right far from the\Nthreshold, and poor near it.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:42.50,0:54:45.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A smooth machine rounds\Noff a steep change.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:45.67,0:54:49.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The bigger tests,\Nin three dimensions and in the
Dialogue: 0,0:54:49.09,0:54:52.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}shapes of real machines,\Nare written as bets.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:54.10,0:54:55.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One lesson remains.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:55.56,0:54:58.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Enforce each law where it is true.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:58.17,0:55:00.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Exactly, where it is exact.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:00.25,0:55:03.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}{\c&H00A1ED&}On average{\c&HF7F4F2&}, where it\Nonly holds on average.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:07.35,0:55:11.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The report, the code,\Nand every result in this film are
Dialogue: 0,0:55:11.50,0:55:15.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}on the project's site,\Nwith their limits written out.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:15.81,0:55:19.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The rest of the staircase\Nis still to be computed.
